#!/usr/bin/python3 -I
# infra/core-law-setup — run once by the governor, as root (ADR-170 Stage 1).
"""
core-law-setup — create the law's custodian (ADR-170 D2-D6, Stage 1).

Usage (as root; core-deploy runs it after a merge that changes it):

    sudo core-law-setup            # plan only
    sudo core-law-setup --apply    # do it

``--governor`` (default ``core-darek``, the Governor in the register of
``.intent/taxonomies/principal_roles.yaml``) is checked, not granted
anything: the governor approves law by merging it and holds no sudo.

Without ``--apply`` it prints what it would do and changes nothing. Every
step is safe to run again: what is already right is left alone, and the
signing key, once made, is never replaced.

What it does:

 1. the account ``core-law``: system account, no login shell, no password;
 2. ``/opt/core-law/venv``: exactly the wheel core-deploy built for the
    live release (``/opt/core/current``), taken from ``releases/<sha>/dist/``
    and accepted only when its sha256 equals ``wheel_sha256`` in that
    release's ``core-release.json``; every dependency hash-pinned from the
    ``poetry.lock`` core-deploy kept beside it (read through core's git when
    the release was built; the runtime ``main`` group only). Nothing comes
    from the development tree or a PyPI release of core-runtime: the custodian
    runs the code the services run. The venv gets a copy of
    ``core-release.json``, so an audit can compare it with the live release.
    Binary wheels only (installing a wheel runs no package code). Owned by
    root, so core-law cannot change the code it runs. Refused when anything
    in it points outside the venv (a ``.pth`` file, an editable install).
    Installed at its final path, because a venv records its own path in its
    entry points; a previous venv is set aside first and put back if the
    new one fails, including when its installed ``core-admin`` does not run
    as core-law. On a fresh host, where nothing is deployed yet, this step
    is skipped and everything else is done: the setup
    order is core-law-setup, core-runtime-setup, core-deploy, then
    core-law-setup again (#1085);
 3. the ledger signing key: generated by core-law in
    ``/opt/core-law/home/.ssh/`` (outside every repository, 0600, core-law
    only); kept on every later run;
 4. governed text in the development tree (``.intent``, ``.specs``,
    ``CLAUDE.md``, ``.claude/settings.json``, ``.claude/hooks``) owned by
    core-law, files 0644 and directories 0755 (no setgid);
 5. the names: the repository root becomes ``root:core-dev 3775`` (sticky),
    so no other account can rename or replace ``.intent``, ``.specs``,
    ``CLAUDE.md`` or ``.claude``; ``.claude`` itself becomes
    ``core-law:core-dev 3775`` so the assistant keeps its own files there.
    Root-level files the assistant commits are handed to ``core-claude`` so
    its git checkout still works (acceptance check 8);
 5a. the repository's parents become root-owned and not writable by others:
    whoever owns a parent (today ``core`` owns ``/opt/dev``) can rename the
    whole repository away and put another tree in its place;
 5b. accounts in a root-equivalent group (``sudo``, ``docker``, ``lxd``,
    ``disk`` ...) are reported: no file permission holds against them. The
    script names them and does not change group membership (the governor's
    act);
 6. ``/opt/core/law/{.intent,.specs}`` (ADR-132 D10.13): seeded once from
    the development tree, owned by core-law, under a root-owned parent;
 6b. ``/opt/core/law/private-names/``: the private-names hook's
    configuration (process-governance-tool's privacy rule), seeded once from
    the assistant's ``~/.config/core/private-names.conf`` and the names file
    it names (only the assistant's own regular files), owned by core-law
    (files 0644): the assistant reads it and cannot change or delete it;
    kept on every later run (governor 2026-10-16);
 7. ``/etc/sudoers.d/core-law`` is removed: the merge is the only approval
    of law and core-law writes it on core-deploy's call, so no account may
    run core-law's ``law apply`` through sudo (ADR-068 amendment 2026-10-15:
    the governor needs no sudo; ADR-170 D6 superseded);
 7a. no law applies itself: ``core-law-auto-apply`` and ``core-law-digest``
    (service and timer) are disabled and deleted when present. Auto-apply
    applied any record-only proposal found in the development tree's
    ``var/law-proposals``, which every ``core-dev`` account can write, so
    ``.specs/`` changed without a merge (2026-10-18). ``law apply`` with the
    typed id is unchanged;
 0. first, a table of every path it touches: owner and mode now, and after;
 8. the acceptance probes: as core-claude, core and core-darek, writing,
    renaming or replacing governed text, or renaming the repository, is
    denied; core-claude can still
    replace its own root-level files. A probe that wrongly succeeds is
    undone at once and reported.

It never runs git and never runs code from the development tree. The only
package code it runs is the released core-admin, as core-law, for a help
check.

Exit codes: 0 done (or plan printed); 1 refused or a probe failed.
"""

